  "body": "What happens: `.github/workflows/post-merge.yml` runs the full batch gate on every push to `main` (`on: push: branches: [main]`, no paths filter). A merge that changes only docs (`site/`, `docs/`, `*.md`) or process files (`.claude/`) starts a full build and test run (30-60 min on a self-hosted runner) that tests exactly the code of the commit before it.\n\nToday this is worked around outside the repo: a script on one collaborator's machine (`pm-cancel`, every 20 min) cancels such runs after they have started. That spends up to 20 runner-minutes per docs merge, depends on one machine, and other forks don't have it.\n\nExpected: a commit that changes no code does not start (or immediately skips) the batch gate, and says so in the run summary.\n\nOptions:\n1. `paths-ignore` on the push trigger (`site/**`, `docs/**`, `**/*.md`, `.claude/**`), plus `workflow_dispatch` for a forced run. Simple; GitHub then shows no run for that commit.\n2. A first step in the existing `yield` job (beside `post-merge-skip.sh`) that diffs the commit against its parent and sets `skip=true` when no file outside those paths changed, with a notice \"docs/process only - the previous run covers this code\". The run exists and is green-skipped, which keeps \"one run per commit\" visible.\n\nOption 2 fits the existing skip mechanism and Sarge's \"one run per commit\" (#3714) best. Either way a commit that touches code, `Cargo.*`, `xtask/`, `assets/`, `.github/` or build scripts must always run the gate.\n\nDone means: a docs-only or process-only merge to main finishes its post-merge workflow in under a minute with a \"skipped: no code changed\" notice; any commit touching code or build inputs runs the full gate as today; the off-repo cancel script is no longer needed.\n",
